<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juho Kusti Paasikivi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as J. K. Paasikivi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juho Kusti Paasikivi (1870–1956) was a Finnish statesman who served as prime minister in 1944–1946 and as president from 1946 to 1956. A conservative banker and veteran negotiator with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, who led the Finnish delegation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> in 1920 and in the talks before the Winter War, he built the post-war policy of neutrality and trust with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the so-called Paasikivi line, sealed by the treaty of 1948 and continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urho-kekkonen">Urho Kekkonen</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juho-kusti-paasikivi.jpg" alt="Juho Kusti Paasikivi" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: F.E Fremling · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J.K._Paasikivi_1945_portrait_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Paasikivi was born Johan Gustaf Hellsten on 27 November 1870 in Hämeenkoski, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, to a travelling merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampere">Tampere</a>. His mother died when he was four, and his father died in debt when he was 14. He took the Finnish name Paasikivi in 1885, entered the University of Helsinki in 1890 and received a doctorate in law in 1902. He was Director-in-Chief of the Treasury of the Grand Duchy of Finland until 1914, and he sat in Parliament for the Finnish Party, which supported greater autonomy but opposed steps the Russians might perceive as aggressive.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After 1914 Paasikivi became chief general manager of the Kansallis-Osake-Pankki bank. After the Bolshevik October Revolution he championed full independence in the form of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> he stood on the side of the White government, and as prime minister from May to November 1918 he sought a German prince, Frederick Charles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a>, as king. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat the monarchy was dropped for a republic and his Senate resigned. In 1934 he became chairman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-coalition-party">National Coalition Party</a>.</p><p>From 1936 to 1939 he was envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>, seeking Swedish defence guarantees, which did not come. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> he represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in the negotiations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and supported compliance with some of the demands. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-11-november-1918">armistice</a> and peace talks, and resigned as envoy in Moscow when he learned that thoughts in Helsinki ran towards retaking lost territory with German aid. By 1943 he had concluded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would lose the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Paasikivi became prime minister in 1944, and Parliament chose him president in 1946, at the age of 75, when Mannerheim resigned. His policy was realistic: he held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> must adapt to superpower politics and sign treaties with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and he aimed to prove that Finland posed no threat to it. In spring 1948 he ordered army and navy units to Helsinki against a possible communist coup, and after the elections of that year he kept the communists out of government. His approach, called the Paasikivi doctrine, was named Finlandization in the 1970s.</p><p>He was re-elected in 1950 and opened the 1952 Summer Olympics in Helsinki. By the end of his second term the Karelian refugees had been resettled, the war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> paid and rationing ended, and in January 1956 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> removed its troops from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porkkala">Porkkala</a> naval base. He died on 14 December 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juho Kusti Paasikivi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1944 President <b>Juho Kusti Paasikivi</b> and his successor Urho Kekkonen, president from 1956 to 1982, pursued neutrality combined with careful friendship with Moscow under the treaty of 1948, a policy critics abroad called Finlandisation; Kekkonen’s long rule concentrated great power in the presidency.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hämeenkoski, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. Died in Helsinki, in what is now <a href="/countries/finland">Finland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=61.03,25.15&amp;year=1925" aria-label="Hämeenkoski on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5071 128 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Juho Kusti Paasikivi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 398h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 398h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=61.03,25.15&amp;year=1925">What was here in 1925? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
